{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfUcT4RAZ25ZkfQ6NP5X9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `/app/sched.py` is complete and verified.\n\n## Summary\n\n**What I learned from profiling (5.6 GPU-s of the 8.3 budget):**\n- Step count per segment follows `steps = S_min · (1 + B_crit / B)` with `S_min ≈ 0.0242`, and per-step time is `t_overhead + B/throughput` (≈0.96 s + B/475). So the time-optimal batch is `B* = sqrt(B_crit · t_overhead · throughput)`.\n- Phase 1: `B_crit` ramps only mildly (~36 → ~60), giving an optimal batch of ~130–180 — far below the production constant of 512.\n- The corpus switch is a sudden jump in `B_crit` (×4 to ×40 depending on the replica, e.g. ~1800 on the profile replica, ~180 and ~470 on two evaluate replicas) with `S_min` unchanged, followed by a slow drift. Optimal phase-2 batch is therefore run-specific (≈300–1200).\n\n**The scheduler (`/app/sched.py`, stdlib only):**\n- Phase 1: uses the fixed, profiled `B_crit` ramp to pick `B*` per segment.\n- Switch detection: if a segment's realised steps exceed 1.25× the phase-1 prediction (noise is ~5%, so this is a >5σ event), it flags the switch immediately.\n- Phase 2: re-estimates `B_crit` from every observed step count, fits a damped linear trend over the last 8 estimates, extrapolates one segment ahead, and picks `B*` (clamped to `[b_min, b_max]`). Always makes exactly 40 `run_segment` calls.\n\n**Verification:** `lab evaluate` draws a fresh replica each call (its printed `best_constant_batch_seconds` of 3.781 is a fixed number, so per-call scores vary wildly). Traces from two evaluates confirmed correct behaviour end to end — switch caught in the very segment it occurred (at 19 and at 31), phase-2 batch converging within one segment — and results of 3.04 s, 2.09 s, and 2.13 s on the replicas where the switch was modest; the higher totals (3.95 s, 5.85 s) correspond to replicas with expensive switches, where a cons