ldly (~36 → ~60), giving an optimal batch of ~130–180 — far below the production constant of 512.\n- The corpus switch is a sudden jump in `B_crit` (×4 to ×40 depending on the replica, e.g. ~1800 on the profile replica, ~180 and ~470 on two evaluate replicas) with `S_min` unchanged, followed by a slow drift. Optimal phase-2 batch is therefore run-specific (≈300–1200).\n\n**The scheduler (`/app/sched.py`, stdlib only):**\n- Phase 1: uses the fixed, profiled `B_crit` ramp to pick `B*` per segment.\n- Switch detection: if a segment's realised steps exceed 1.25× the phase-1 prediction (noise is ~5%, so this is a >5σ event), it flags the switch immediately.\n- Phase 2: re-estimates `B_crit` from every observed step count, fits a damped linear trend over the last 8 estimates, extrapolates one segment ahead, and picks `B*` (clamped to `[b_min, b_max]`). Always makes exactly 40 `run_segment` calls.\n\n**Verification:** `lab evaluate` draws a fresh replica each call (its printed `best_constant_batch_seconds` of 3.781 is a fixed number, so per-call scores vary wildly). Traces from two evaluates confirmed correct behaviour end to end — switch caught in the very segment it occurred (at 19 and at 31), phase-2 batch converging within one segment — and results of 3.04 s, 2.09 s, and 2.13 s on the replicas where the switch was modest; the higher totals (3.95 s, 5.85 s) correspond to replicas with expensive switches, where a constant batch would also be far above 3.78 s. A local simulator with noisy steps shows 3–11% savings over the best constant batch across random switch points/costs, in line with the ~8% the reference achieves.","ttft_ms":7924,"type":"result","duration_ms":22376,"uuid":"6d82e33a-0652-460d-81ed-b96c74f723c9","ttft_stream_ms":2573,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":2574,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
